docs/CURRENT_STATE.md:45:**Clean Step 1 source investigation (2026-11-07):** A [source-loss census](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-LOSS-CENSUS.md) traces two separate losses in the historical cleaner and records reproducible corpus observations. Offline candidate comparisons expose both recovered content and source-identity or contamination risks. A subsequent [full-page diagnostic](../clean-arch/STEP1-FULL-PAGE-RESULTS.md) completed 102 calls on 17 selected articles and independent source-backed review. It exposes examples of both missing or misattributed source text and model errors that persist despite the right text being present. A Codex–Claude council is reviewing the results and earlier attempts; no source-cleaning candidate is adopted, and the source corpus and production defaults are unchanged.
docs/CURRENT_STATE.md:49:**Clean architecture Step 1 (2026-11-06, isolated work on `clean-arch`):** Alex reopened work with the clean architecture. An experimental prepared-source handoff now preserves the archived page, prior cleaned text, source-role decisions and selected-line mapping without changing the original database; quotations for prepared items must also occur contiguously in the original captured page. Two seven-item runs are available in the [clean architecture PRD](../clean-arch/PRD.md), one in the earlier selection order and one in recorded page-timestamp order. The source selector is not a production service and source ordering is not established event-arrival ordering. The runs expose unresolved semantic problems: citations can omit supporting facts despite an exact location match; model output can change a benchmark into a deployment, reverse a backup relation, omit a named customer or assign an unstated currency. A blinded 84-call check-placement/wording comparison and a 27-call same-article citation-completion diagnostic did not give a clean semantic fix and are not adopted. The latter missed an omitted connectivity condition and a reversed backup relationship, and left six expected evidence reviews unaddressed. A further five-round Codex–Claude council produced a narrowly scoped Step 1 link candidate, independently checked for prompt-contract drift. A six-article, 36-call blind comparison completed; the candidate still produced an explicitly self-contradictory link and both arms consistently omitted the same Kyber report and company response. It is not adopted and has not changed the active PRD prompt. A trace confirms that both the Kyber report and open Kyber thesis were supplied, and the raw model answers omitted the report and denial; the reason is not established. See ../poc/data/clean-step1-kyber-trace-2026-11-06/TRACE.md. An isolated six-call comparison showed that hiding the Kyber thesis did not recover the report or denial in any draw. A second six-call comparison returned them in 3/3 draws with the original Kyber paragraph alone and 0/3 with the full article; length, position and surrounding content changed together, so the reason is not isolated. Excerpt outputs have source-attribution limitations. Neither experiment changed state or established a production fix. (Superseded: the next task then was a whole-article coverage approach; the current next task is under "One next task".) See ../clean-arch/STEP1-IMPLEMENTATION-COUNCIL.md. The earlier four-round council agreed that no tested semantic fix is ready; its next investigation is a source-backed review of the 49-item historical run and its 160 evidence records, with overlapping issue flags and separate article-level omissions, then a separate comparison of the seven-item replays. See ../clean-arch/STEP1-COUNCIL-REVIEW.md. Preserve relevant evidence while identifying the next intervention; production/frozen history remains as described below. See [Step 1 run log](../clean-arch/STEP1-RUN-LOG.md) and [English reading page](../clean-arch/architecture.html).
docs/CURRENT_STATE.md:65:**Simple new-question diagnostic (2026-11-05):** At Alex's direction, an isolated prompt returned new questions plus article evidence without asking for product database fields. Four previously seen real dev articles each ran once: three returned one candidate, one returned none, all four quotes matched their source, and the gold-listed GPU backstop amount question was not proposed. Whether that omission is a new-topic miss depends on whether the existing library covers the topic. Two other candidates require source and independent-significance review; the actual experimental snapshot already had a Kyber topic, so its pass follows Alex's topic-level rule; whether its exact release question captures the shipping milestone remains unverified. This is not a production or full-pipeline acceptance. Details: `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/simple-new-question-results.zh.md`.
docs/CURRENT_STATE.md:71:**Split extraction small test (2026-11-05):** At Alex's request, the isolated `hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review` worktree ran two real golden-v5 articles once through each split lane. An independent model reader found 7 of 19 helpful gold matters fully retained, two partial, two written then dropped, eight missed, and one retained gold not-kept matter. Three saved v108 draws on these articles retained 7, 9 and 12 of 19, but used 22 seeded questions while the new test used 8/10-question snapshots; these are not directly comparable. The experiment remains unaccepted; see `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/small-scored-test-20261105.md` and `reports/2026-11-05-split-small-test.html`.
docs/CURRENT_STATE.md:83:The small 2026-11-05 study used three archived NVDA articles and an 8/28 historical 68-question snapshot: without versus with an appended report, independent AI review found 14/27 versus 13/27 complete matters. This measured appended extraction context, not the distinct former coverage-start step; D370 removes both by Alex's decision. Full traces and limitations are in the separate `hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review` worktree under `docs/research/2026-11-05-real-input-evidence/existing-library-results-2026-11-05.md`.
docs/CURRENT_STATE.md:86:**One-day smoke completed (2026-11-05 UTC):** Alex requested a quick 1–2 day end-to-end test. One day, 2026-08-29, ran in `/Users/lchingen/hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/poc/data/v117-one-day-smoke-2026-11-05/`: config v117, an 8/28 historical library, 40 extraction calls, 93 evidence records written, four new questions, 54 judged and zero terminal failures. Two judgement attempts were refused and corrected. A Microsoft revenue forecast of $391.1bn was assigned to a rung whose upper bound excludes $391.1bn; the same article's actual quarterly revenue and next-quarter guidance were dropped with no home. Some source dates and bodies are not verified, and an independent 14-article sample review was incomplete due to truncated text. This is not semantic or production acceptance. Findings and scope: `docs/research/2026-11-05-real-input-evidence/one-day-v117-smoke-2026-11-05.zh.md` in that worktree. The clean source-text comparison and its condition-hidden review are complete; council discussion of next steps is ongoing.
docs/CURRENT_STATE.md:90:**Clean Step 1 coverage council (2026-11-06):** Codex and Claude Code independently reviewed the existing attempts and completed three discussion rounds. The proposed next research candidate moves the existing evidence-reading block ahead of the thesis-opening block while preserving the article, open-thesis state and paired output. It is a hypothesis, not an implemented or validated fix. A complete prompt/contract audit and independent review precede any controlled Luna comparison. Historical paragraph-based and source-first experiments, their noise and attribution problems, and the rejected overlap-based merge are recorded in [the council review](../clean-arch/STEP1-COVERAGE-COUNCIL.md). No new Luna experiment was run in the council.
docs/CURRENT_STATE.md:96:**Clean Step 1 fidelity review (2026-11-07):** All 49 saved source packets and 160 original evidence items have received a source-and-record review; focused independent cross-checks cover the findings and selected controls. The [review and article replay](../clean-arch/STEP1-FIDELITY-REVIEW.md) distinguish source contamination and missing source material from model meaning, citation, date and link issues. The [isolated schema property-order diagnostic](../clean-arch/STEP1-CITATION-ORDER-RESULTS.md) completed all 66 calls and condition-blinded article reviews. Several omissions and semantic errors persisted; it did not establish a fix and is not adopted. It used saved articles and before-states without changing production.
docs/CURRENT_STATE.md:98:**Clean Step 1 source handoff council (2026-11-07):** The ongoing [council and offline checks](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-HANDOFF-COUNCIL.md) reconstructed 16 matching article-34 controls, all omitting a Kyber report and denial; the cause remains unknown. The 20-article saved source-role overlap review distinguished previously cited AI-generated summary lines and an unrelated recommendation from authored body. The clean runner does not send the saved source-role labels; the council chose to first compare original and previously selected source text on ten known articles. The isolated 60-call source-text comparison and condition-hidden review are complete; [results](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-TEXT-RESULTS.md) show specific contamination reductions alongside persistent omissions and changed meaning. It did not advance state or write production. The isolated six-article low-versus-medium source-fidelity diagnostic completed 36/36 calls and condition-hidden review. [Results](../clean-arch/STEP1-REASONING-FIDELITY-RESULTS.md) show partial improvements alongside persistent source-qualification and attribution concerns; no production setting is changed. The [prompt audit](../clean-arch/STEP1-FIDELITY-PROMPT-AUDIT.md) maps the observed failures to the full request and signed standard. The six-article isolated schema-description comparison completed 36/36 calls and condition-hidden review; the candidate did not preserve the repeated example qualification and is not adopted. [Results](../clean-arch/STEP1-SUMMARY-FIDELITY-RESULTS.md). A further six-article 36-call low-versus-medium comparison used corpus-cleaned sources outside the original 49-item clean replay and a fixed exploratory context; results were mixed, including unsupported links and incorrect attribution/date fields. [Results and replay](../clean-arch/STEP1-ADDITIONAL-SOURCES-RESULTS.md). The structured-claims candidate was not sent after review revealed scope and complexity concerns. Council review of the next action is ongoing. The date-text schema-description diagnostic completed 48/48 calls on eight known cases, with mixed results; the candidate is not adopted. A selected-run census found twelve literal "null" strings in date-text fields, but no production writer impact is established. [Results and replay](../clean-arch/STEP1-DATE-DESCRIPTION-RESULTS.md). A further preregistered eight-source date comparison completed 48/48 calls; the candidate lost a supported company-article statement date in all three draws on one source and is not adopted. [Results and replay](../clean-arch/STEP1-DATE-NEW-SOURCES-RESULTS.md). A bounded corpus census confirmed 163 source pages across three explicit generated-summary structures whose labels were removed while some summary text was retained; two NVIDIA cases were reproduced through the cleaner. [Source provenance investigation](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-PROVENANCE-CENSUS.md). A read-only source-lineage replay reproduced 363/363 cleaned pages with current inputs, preserved ambiguous original-line locations, and surfaced the generated-summary label loss for review. [Source investigation and lineage replay](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-PROVENANCE-CENSUS.md). An offline neighboring-context observation exposed only 144 or 149 of 163 known labels and introduced many other page lines; it is not adopted as a general source fix. [Observations and viewer](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-CONTEXT-OBSERVATIONS.md). A new [saved-quote integrity investigation](../clean-arch/STEP1-QUOTE-INTEGRITY-CENSUS.md) reconstructed 360 saved answers across eight overlapping cohorts, including 78 prepared-source calls. Five saved unverified passage instances were complete matches under the existing normalization; an in-memory candidate recovered their original slices, while ten others remained unverified. The normalized source-slice lookup is now installed only in the isolated clean Step 1 runner; saved-answer and entry-point checks are recorded there. Page provenance, Unicode completeness, production resource budgets and writer integration remain open. The existing writer stores only one quote, whereas 63 of 160 evidence items in the 49-article exploratory trace have multiple passages. A [source-first 49-article coverage review](../clean-arch/STEP1-COVERAGE-CENSUS.md) separates material missing from the model input from source-visible omissions in the saved answers, with uncertain matter boundaries labeled. (Superseded: the next investigation then was why supplied material is missed; the current next task is under "One next task".) The canonical prompt and production configuration remain unchanged. Adding fallible role annotations remains a separate untested candidate. Council streams and handoffs are in `poc/data/clean-step1-ongoing-council-2026-11-07/`.
clean-arch/PRD.md:35:    W --> J[Step 4: judge affected theses]
clean-arch/PRD.md:52:| Step 4 | The affected thesis and its saved evidence. | A thesis judgement; the detailed contract remains open. |
clean-arch/PRD.md:83:These are two possible results from the same reading, not two required calls. Every proposed evidence statement is paired with an existing or proposed new thesis, and a new thesis is proposed with evidence. An existing thesis retains its identity; finding evidence for it does not require renaming it. The Step 1 prompt should define duplicate theses clearly so the model can use an existing thesis when the idea is already covered; the precise definition still needs to be worked out and evaluated.
clean-arch/PRD.md:110:The first forward run can advance a separate, isolated test thesis library using those proposals. It can review Luna's output and the evolving thesis state without creating production-compatible judgement records. This test library must not be presented as proof that the existing ledger writer or Step 4 can consume the result.
clean-arch/PRD.md:112:On 2026-11-06, Alex agreed that a new thesis should carry the entity, research question and outcome being tracked, with a period when known. For this single-entity flow, code supplies the entity from the input; Luna supplies the question, outcome and known period. The proposed `period` field is null when the period is unknown. Whether Step 4 needs predefined outcome ranges is a separate open decision; this minimum is not sufficient to claim compatibility with the current production writer.
clean-arch/PRD.md:141:A match outside the returned set will not be considered by the comparison. Search quality therefore matters independently from the model's ability to compare the records it receives.
clean-arch/PRD.md:167:**Repeat handoff:** when Jev identifies a repeat, the comparison must retain the identity of the existing evidence record it was compared against. Step 3 uses that identity to place the incoming record in the existing record's group, adding its source and any missing thesis link. The typed Jev response need not generate the record ID: the caller can associate the choice with the candidate it supplied. The rule for several matching candidates is stated above.
clean-arch/PRD.md:189:A revision does not erase the earlier statement. On 2026-11-06, Alex chose to keep one evidence record for a repeat and add the new source and any missing thesis link. On 2026-11-08 the Step 2 validation showed that keeping only the earlier text discards details a later report adds, such as a guidance condition; Alex then chose to keep each repeat's own text and source inside the stored record's group, without rewriting the original ("A 继续；跑council loop 开始做"; [Step 2 log](step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md) §7). How a judgement reads a group is being tested. Multiple articles reporting the same statement do not automatically establish independent original sources. The current production writer stores separate repeat records, so this is a change for the clean path and needs implementation and verification; the existing historical records remain available. Step 3 ends when the chosen thesis, evidence and relationships are saved. How they are physically stored and written is a separate implementation decision.
clean-arch/PRD.md:191:## 7. Step 4: judge the thesis
clean-arch/PRD.md:236:A separate model-assisted review was tested on nine saved, known source-and-evidence packets, with three calls per packet and two independent readers. All three Firmus reviews found the missing US$2 billion fundraising passage, but all three FEV reviews missed the source passage for limited or unavailable internet. All three also missed a different FEV record’s reversed local-versus-cloud backup relationship; only two of three identified an unsupported US-dollar currency on the Firmus valuation. Six of 69 expected evidence reviews were left unaddressed because returned identifiers did not match the evidence identifiers. These are selected examples, not a quality rate. [The detailed review](../poc/data/clean-step1-prepared-2026-11-06/citation-completion-probe/BLIND-REVIEW.md) keeps the source-backed examples and limitations.
clean-arch/PRD.md:248:Codex and Claude Code reviewed the current source, prompt, code and saved answers, then challenged each other over four rounds. Their [detailed council record](STEP1-COUNCIL-REVIEW.md) links to each exchange and distinguishes observed errors from unproven causes. Claude could see earlier experiment reviews during its first pass, so this was not a blind independent assessment.
clean-arch/PRD.md:252:The agreed next investigation is a source-backed review of the 49-item historical forward run and its 160 returned evidence records, with separate, overlapping flags and a separate article-level review for omitted facts. Preserve source contamination and incompleteness as distinct findings, and adjudicate difficult cases with another reader. Then compare the two seven-item replays separately: all seven source IDs are already in the 49-item manifest, and their inputs and order differ. Before the full review, check the review method against known problems and independently checked ordinary records. This is an investigation of recorded runs, not a production-quality rate or a change to the prompt.
clean-arch/PRD.md:266:This is a proposed clarification, **not a verified fix**: the current request already implies much of the same behavior. Relevant context, counterevidence, multiple valid links and meaningful new topics must remain. The first comparison reuses six stored article requests and their actual open-thesis states, with the original request as a same-day control; it will compare all outputs and useful omissions. The prepared request files pass the existing structural checks, but have not been sent to Luna. [The implementation council plan](STEP1-IMPLEMENTATION-COUNCIL.md) records the exact approach, source examples, independent audit, controls and limitations. The earlier 49-item fidelity review remains incomplete.
clean-arch/PRD.md:286:The independent reader first assessed anonymous source and evidence without seeing thesis identities or links. The second pass checked links against the actual input; at that point the condition could be inferred. No thesis state advanced. This result does not support claiming that the mere presence of the existing Kyber thesis caused the omission in this example, and it does not establish the underlying cause. Removing a thesis is a diagnostic only and is not a proposed production change. [The complete trace](../poc/data/clean-step1-kyber-trace-2026-11-06/TRACE.md) preserves the source, self-account, requests and limitations.
clean-arch/PRD.md:318:Codex and Claude Code are examining the source-to-extraction handoff and prior attempts. The original 49 saved requests and seven prepared-source requests had empty author fields; the prepared records retained proposed source roles, but those roles were not sent in the extraction request. Roles can mix bylines, outlets, dates and uncertain page material, so they cannot safely be promoted to an author field. A 20-article overlap check found that previously cited passages excluded by the recorded selections were an unrelated recommendation or explicitly labeled AI summaries; it does not establish full body coverage. Sixteen repeated article-34 controls omitted the same Kyber report and denial; the cause remains unproven. The [council record](STEP1-SOURCE-HANDOFF-COUNCIL.md) links the reproduced comparisons and previous failures. An isolated ten-article comparison completed 60 calls with the same prompt, schema and pre-item thesis states. Prepared text removed a known unrelated-story error in article 3 (2/3 original, 0/3 prepared) and generated-summary citations in article 8 (2/3, 0/3), but did not reliably improve extraction: article 16’s restored financing paragraph was in all three prepared inputs and in none of their answers; article 28’s example model was stated as a selection in all six answers; and article 34’s delay report and denial appeared in 2/3 controls and 0/3 prepared answers despite being present in both inputs. These are selected-case observations, not general rates or established causes. The [full results](STEP1-SOURCE-TEXT-RESULTS.md) and [60-answer replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-text-comparison-2026-11-07/review.html) show source and output. In two retrospective follow-ups, the model recognized that the article 28 wording did not establish a fixed model selection, but could not recover its original reasoning; the article 16 follow-up did not identify the financing paragraph. These accounts are diagnostic, not a fix. No production change is adopted. An isolated low-versus-medium reasoning comparison on six known prepared articles completed and was reviewed with setting labels hidden. Both settings stated an example model as actually used in all three article-28 answers. In article 29, stock-market flows were blurred into infrastructure spending in 3/3 low and 0/3 medium answers, but the medium answers did not retain that stock-flow statement. Other attribution and context concerns remained. These are selected-case observations, not a production acceptance or proof of cause. See the [full results](STEP1-REASONING-FIDELITY-RESULTS.md) and [36-answer replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-reasoning-fidelity-2026-11-07/review.html). The [source-fidelity prompt audit](STEP1-FIDELITY-PROMPT-AUDIT.md) maps observed failures to the complete request and signed standards. The council and an independent reader reviewed a field-local wording hypothesis that asks summaries to preserve source qualifications. In a six-article isolated comparison, all three answers in each arm still described an example model as part of the proposed system without preserving the “such as” qualification; other context and linking concerns remained. The field-description candidate is not adopted. [Full results](STEP1-SUMMARY-FIDELITY-RESULTS.md) and [36-answer replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-summary-fidelity-2026-11-07/review.html) preserve the cases and limits; the council is investigating the next step.
clean-arch/PRD.md:320:The continuing council has reviewed a possible structured representation of source statements and link explanations within the same extraction call. It is an untested diagnostic, not an adopted design or demonstrated fix. A prior reviewer missed a reversed relationship even with the full article and one evidence record; the proposed representation must still preserve legitimate inference, multiple sources and existing pairs. An independent audit found that the first draft could exclude the very link explanation being investigated, and the council chose not to run this enlarged diagnostic as the next test. A further low-versus-medium comparison completed on six manually selected real articles outside the original 49-item replay, with one fixed 22-thesis exploratory state for both settings. These use the corpus cleaned text, not the earlier prepared-source path; their prior exposure outside the clean replay is unknown. Earlier mixed results and regressions remain unresolved. The [council record](STEP1-SOURCE-HANDOFF-COUNCIL.md) and [draft experiment](../poc/data/clean-step1-structured-claims-2026-11-07/design.md) preserve the discussion, offline checks and limits.
clean-arch/PRD.md:322:The additional-source comparison produced mixed results: both settings retained several central claims, while the higher setting also produced unsupported CPU-related links and date-field errors in individual answers. An independent review also found 13 fields across eight repeated answers that appear to use an event or data period as the original statement date; ambiguous cases were kept separate. In three later, specifically prompted continuations, the model correctly distinguished those time references; this does not establish the original cause or a fix. A quotation found in the cleaned input was not contiguous in the captured original page. These are selected-case observations, not population rates or a reason to change the production setting. [Results](STEP1-ADDITIONAL-SOURCES-RESULTS.md) and [article-by-article replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-reasoning-new-sources-2026-11-07/review.html) show the source, context, answers and limitations.
clean-arch/PRD.md:326:An isolated date diagnostic compared one revised `statement_date_text` schema description with the saved request on eight known source/context pairs. It leaves all schema types, evidence pairing, prompt body and production configuration unchanged. Controls include a third-party statement independently dated to the article day, explicit announcements and a dated party release. Independent review identified risks of losing valid metadata-only or announcement dates; these were inspected alongside omissions and linkage. The results were mixed, including persistent date mistakes, unsupported-link concerns and literal `"null"` text in two fields; the candidate is not adopted. [Results](STEP1-DATE-DESCRIPTION-RESULTS.md) and [48-answer replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-date-description-2026-11-07/review.html) retain the details. The [design](../poc/data/clean-step1-date-description-2026-11-07/design.md) records the exact scope and prior attempts.
clean-arch/PRD.md:330:A further isolated comparison completed on eight new source IDs selected before reading article contents using a recorded seed and a structured inventory of available clean-Step-1 artifacts. It reuses the same unmodified date-text candidate and the same fixed exploratory thesis context for both arms. The selection cannot prove absence from all past work or exclude syndicated stories under different IDs. An independent source review found related-story contamination and unlabeled generated summaries in some cleaned inputs; these were assessed separately. One company article lost its supported full statement date in all three candidate answers, while another preserved its date in both arms; the candidate is not adopted. [Results and replay](STEP1-DATE-NEW-SOURCES-RESULTS.md) and the [selection design](../poc/data/clean-step1-date-unseen-2026-11-07/design.md) retain the evidence.
clean-arch/PRD.md:332:An independent [thesis-link review](STEP1-ROUTING-CENSUS.md) examined all 214 links across the eight preregistered articles. It records unsupported topic bridges and explanations that negate their own link, alongside valid indirect context and source-supported prospective topics. These are selected-case findings, not population error rates or a hardcoded relevance filter.
clean-arch/PRD.md:334:A [source-provenance census](STEP1-SOURCE-PROVENANCE-CENSUS.md) found 163 records across three explicitly recognizable page components where generated-summary content was retained but its label was removed. This is a bounded source-structure count, not a count of model errors. On two NVIDIA pages, the cleaner's template removal and long-line restoration reproduce the mismatch; a general provenance-preserving handoff is under review. An offline probe reproduced the current cleaned text for 163 affected pages and 200 seed-selected additional pages. It retains all candidate original positions, marks transformed bylines, and shows one missing-HTML fallback; positions do not by themselves establish source role. [Lineage replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-lineage-2026-11-07/review.html). Source content remains available and no production data or prompt has been changed.
clean-arch/PRD.md:338:A [saved quote-integrity census](STEP1-QUOTE-INTEGRITY-CENSUS.md) independently matched 360 answers in eight overlapping cohorts to their original requests and results, and reconstructed a further 60-answer source-text comparison separately. It distinguishes exact page location, unverified quotations and cleaned-text-only joins. Five unverified passage instances are full matches under the existing normalizer, including spaces before punctuation. The isolated Step 1 runner now locates a complete normalized match when every matching source slice has the same original bytes. It retains the model quotation, displays the located source text, and records lookup errors without stopping the exploratory stream. The [installed-path review](STEP1-QUOTE-INTEGRITY-CENSUS.md) covers saved-answer replays, Unicode and duplicate controls, resource checks and limitations. This fixes a narrow location issue in the isolated runner; semantic accuracy, original-page provenance and production compatibility remain unproven.
clean-arch/PRD.md:348:The investigation also found that the historical extractor-matching key removes non-ASCII characters and can accidentally match an empty key. Two Unicode-aware offline alternatives had different effects on the saved articles, including adding unrelated page titles; neither is adopted. A proposed punctuation change recovered the financing paragraph but also added serialized HTML and navigation on other pages. The [investigation](STEP1-SOURCE-LOSS-CENSUS.md) records the full-corpus comparisons and their limits. Source identity and article boundaries need to be evaluated alongside text matching.
clean-arch/PRD.md:354:Two independent readers have completed [source-first notes for the 17 proposed articles](STEP1-FULL-PAGE-SOURCE-NOTES.md), before any new comparison outputs. These notes identify source roles, incomplete captures, uncertain statements and later page material; they are not a new golden set or mandatory extraction checklist.
clean-arch/PRD.md:356:The isolated full-page comparison completed all **102 calls** across 17 articles, with three answers for each input per article. Three independent readers examined the answers against the source, with imperfect condition hiding. In one selected article the page exposed an outlook qualification absent from the cleaned input, and all three page answers included it; in another, all three cleaned-input answers misattributed an unrelated later-story teaser, while the page answers did not. In a third, five of six answers turned an example model into a definite selection despite both inputs preserving the qualification. The [source-backed results](STEP1-FULL-PAGE-RESULTS.md) distinguish source loss, attribution and model meaning errors, and the [article-by-article replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-full-page-diagnostic-2026-11-07/review.html) shows the inputs and answers. These selected cases do not establish a general accuracy rate or production readiness.
clean-arch/PRD.md:362:The isolated candidate has now been built and its 34,084 records compared with the two source snapshots. A fake-answer run through the actual Step 1 runner confirms that the previously excluded CNBC article reaches the runner with its saved page. Replays also preserve the recorded validation results for 102 saved comparison answers and 49 completed forward-run answers. This verifies the handoff and historical checks, not model extraction or article identity. The [council regroup](STEP1-SOURCE-REGROUP-COUNCIL.md) records why source cleanup comes first for a stable test and which model-processing errors remain separate.
clean-arch/PRD.md:366:The [control-run design](STEP1-CAPTURE-CONTROLS-DESIGN.md) sends four eligible captured pages independently with one fixed hypothetical thesis state, plus one comparison using the CNBC article's previous cleaned text. It preserves the existing extraction request and treats missing article content, source attribution and model interpretation as separate questions. All five calls completed; the [replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-capture-controls-2026-11-07/review.html) shows the actual inputs and proposals. In these selected draws, the wrong page yielded no pairs, while the two partial pages yielded qualified proposals. Source review also found summary details whose supporting headline or article passage was not included in the attached quotations. The [results](STEP1-CAPTURE-CONTROLS-RESULTS.md) record the evidence and remaining uncertainty; these draws are not a general quality measurement. A [request audit](STEP1-CITATION-SUPPORT-AUDIT.md) confirms that the current prompt already asks for all passages needed to support each summary; the cause of these omissions is not established.
clean-arch/PRD.md:370:An independent [citation review of all 160 returned records](STEP1-CITATION-COMPLETENESS-49.md) in the historical 49-article run found nine preliminary cases where details appear in the supplied article but not in their own evidence passages. A second reader checked those cases, distinguishing substantive missing conditions from source-identity or contextual antecedents. Meaning changes and whole-article omissions are separate problems, measured in different units; this selected run does not establish which problem is largest in production.
clean-arch/PRD.md:372:The council is also examining whether selecting source-unit IDs could reduce copying errors. An [offline boundary and size check](STEP1-SOURCE-ID-OBSERVATIONS.md) found that the existing quote-repair boundary heuristic splits real abbreviations and that many saved quotations cross its units; a simple numbered JSON representation also increased source-only input size on the checked pages. IDs would not establish semantic support or article identity. No such representation is adopted.
clean-arch/PRD.md:386:Codex and Claude Code (`claude-fable-5-1`) reviewed the 49 source records, original requests and answers, state, and the clean runner over three rounds. They challenged each other's claims against the saved records and code. Claude read the earlier Codex review before completing its first pass through all 49 articles, so this was **not a fully blind independent review**. The [first](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/claude-round1.md), [second](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/claude-round2.md) and [third](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/claude-round3.md) responses, [Codex's checks](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/CODEX-OBSERVATIONS.md), and local packets are retained for inspection. These are findings and proposed investigations, not approved changes to the architecture or production behavior.
clean-arch/PRD.md:390:| 1. Wrong thesis links | In article 4, valuation and revenue are linked to Nemotron performance; in article 27, a cloud-gaming server is linked to the Shield TV thesis. Three of the 199 returned links explicitly say the item does not inform the selected thesis. Article 18 puts robotics evidence under the Nemotron/NemoClaw thesis; article 41 links general networking details to a specific Kyber setback. One 2027 roadmap statement in article 41 can also be defensible context for that thesis. | Luna returned these links; the clean runner checks reference identity and source location, not whether the link answers the thesis. That separation is deliberate: code does not decide importance. The cause of the model's decision is **not proven**. A forced pairing or insufficient deliberation may contribute; neither is established by one run. | Compare matching decisions against the complete question and outcome, including valid indirect links. Preserve the user's thesis–evidence pairing rule. Do not add a hard-coded list of keywords or make code decide investment relevance. |
clean-arch/PRD.md:400:**First no-cost source test, completed as an investigation, not a replacement pipeline:** compare saved HTML, the structural extractor and the current cleaned text for article 3 and article 28, with article 34 as a positive control for real text that the fallback preserves. Count both unrelated content retained and relevant article content lost before proposing a change. The [candidate census](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/source_fallback_census.py) found 112 long retained lines across 23 of these 49 records that are absent from the extractor output; these are candidates, **not 112 proven errors**. The [timestamp census](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/timestamp_census.py) and raw outputs record its scope and limitations.
clean-arch/PRD.md:413:A separate model-assisted source-preparation experiment asked Luna to identify article lines in the archived whole-page text without rewriting them. One draw on six known pages recovered the real body paragraphs checked in articles 2, 3, 16, 28, 34 and 42, including the previously missing CFO and funding statements; it excluded the identified unrelated recommendations in articles 3, 28 and 34. An independent reader checked the selected text against the page and HTML. But article 2's selected text presents the page's AI-generated summary without its label, and also includes a photo caption and credit. Articles 3 and 16 lose visible author names while the stored author field is empty. All six returned no uncertain lines. Each selected line was copied unchanged from the stored normalized page, with no invalid or repeated IDs; exact copying does not solve source attribution. These six selected pages and one draw do not establish production safety, quality across the source pool, or completeness of a paywalled article.
clean-arch/PRD.md:415:A second isolated source-boundary experiment separated article text, bylines, captions, page-generated summaries and uncertain material, allowing a line to have several roles. An independent reviewer compared one draw on ten selected pages with the saved page and HTML. They found no wholly missing authored body paragraph or visible byline name among those pages, and the explicitly labeled AI summaries were separate from the article role. The CFO and funding statements remained, and the known unrelated recommendations were excluded. Figure captions on two pages were available only in the caption role; article 7's AI-widget disclosure was omitted; and the source of some widget “Next Steps” text is unclear even though the model marked no lines uncertain. A downstream consumer would need the separate source roles and their provenance, not just the article text. These are selected-case observations, not a completeness or accuracy guarantee. A further check used three sends each on ten other articles, randomly selected from the same 49-article corpus. Independent readers found the substantive paragraphs visible in the archived article regions in all 30 outputs, but one output omitted a visible author whose stored `authors` field was empty. Some outlet labels, contributor roles and article/caption roles also require care. The model later identified the missed author when prompted, which does not prove why it missed her initially. These articles appeared in the earlier extraction review, so this is not a fully unseen corpus; the [independent check](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/source-prep-check/INDEPENDENT-REVIEW.md) is not a population accuracy estimate. The [second probe](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/source-prep-probe-v2/) is not integrated into the pipeline.
clean-arch/PRD.md:421:**Status:** the causes above have been traced, but no general source-cleaning or ordering fix is established. Do not silently remove the fallback, switch extractors or invent times for missing records. Compare a candidate against both contamination and genuine-body controls before using it in a new stateful run. The [source fallback census](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/source_fallback_census.json), [headline-filter census](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/headline-filter-census.json), [extractor comparison](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/default-extractor-comparison.json), and [corrected timestamp census](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/timestamp_census_v2.json) preserve the local investigation. The censuses identify candidates, not independently adjudicated error counts.
clean-arch/PRD.md:427:A second reader independently checked eight saved link cases. Across their 41 evidence items and 47 links, the stored links match the raw provider answers; the runner did not choose them. The reader confirmed article 4's two links and article 27's one link explicitly contradict the model's own explanations. Article 18 attaches seven robotics statements to a Nemotron/NemoClaw thesis without a named connection in the item. Article 41 requires care: its general networking facts do not state a connection to the Kyber setback, but its 2027 plan can be relevant context to the roadmap. Article 10's provider commitments and roadmap counter-evidence are examples of links that can legitimately inform more than one question. Source contamination and the original order limit all these observations.
clean-arch/PRD.md:429:For articles 4 and 27, Luna was shown its own stored answer and the original context and asked to review the links. It acknowledged both contradictions. In article 4 it suggested a new, evidence-backed revenue thesis; in article 27 it suggested no pair. It attributed the errors to an overly broad interpretation of indirect relevance. This is a **retrospective account**, not proof of the original cause. The full [requests and responses](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/link-account-probes/) are retained. A controlled exploratory comparison resent eight unchanged saved requests and their original before-states three times at low and three at medium reasoning. Independent readers reviewed anonymous outputs before the settings were revealed. In five selected wrong-link scenarios, 6 of 15 low-setting answers contained at least one clear wrong-product or unrelated-question link, compared with 0 of 15 medium-setting answers in that specific class. Other links and source-fidelity issues remained, and the examples were selected after the original run, so this is not a general accuracy estimate or proof of cause. The 24 low responses used 45,091 completion tokens versus 106,839 for the 24 medium responses; these are usage counts, not a cost or latency comparison. [Blind review and limitations](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/link-setting-probe/BLIND-REVIEW.md) records the cases. The experiment did not advance state or change the production setting. A second isolated test compared the saved prompt with a candidate that makes the existing final check actionable and makes the technical-comparison exclusion explicit. Three independent readers inspected six unlabeled answers for each of three known articles before the prompt labels were revealed. Every candidate and baseline answer still retained excluded technical comparisons, while also retaining the IBM integration, relevant AMD competitor comparison and Perplexity usage in the applicable controls. A candidate answer additionally misstated which company’s hardware was in a 100 kW rack. The candidate is not adopted. This small, selected test does not establish a general rate; the [blind review](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/benchmark-prompt-probe/BLIND-REVIEW.md) records the outputs and limits. Neither test changes the current prompt or database.
clean-arch/PRD.md:433:For topic and evidence noise, article 8's company-reported technical benchmark items and article 33's benchmark conflict with the saved prompt's final check. An independent reader inspected all 160 output items and flagged 13 primarily technical test or comparison items, two mixed items whose non-benchmark content needs separate review, two competitor-comparison controls and six uncertain boundary items. These are item-level leads from one run, not a count of research matters or a general error rate. This does not prove every technical detail is unhelpful. In article 1 the only evidence for the newly opened performance thesis is a company-reported benchmark; excluding that item under the existing standard would leave that proposed thesis without evidence. Its separate adoption thesis has other evidence and must be considered independently. Article 8 also reports IBM production-workload testing and integration, which should not be discarded with the benchmark. A rival's comparison and reported use in article 19, the named robotics integration in article 20, and NVIDIA-backed financing in article 44 require their own relevance judgment. The user's rule remains: an observable, meaningful topic can be opened before its financial impact is known; avoid noise without imposing a blanket size or industry threshold.
clean-arch/PRD.md:491:The precise boundaries between these two theses, and which additional material an analyst would retain from each complete source, need independent review. The exercise illustrates how to save before-state, article, proposed pairs and after-state. It does not establish a finalized golden answer or acceptance rule.
clean-arch/PRD.md:506:6. **Judge separately:** Step 4 may assess the thesis using the evidence. Its decision process is not specified here.
clean-arch/PRD.md:513:2. **New thesis identity:** How should the Step 1 prompt define duplicate theses, and how accurately does the model apply that definition without a separate thesis-routing stage?
clean-arch/PRD.md:518:7. **Judgement:** What evidence does Step 4 use, when does it run, and what does it produce?
clean-arch/PRD.md:553:The weak point is link clutter: a blind recheck of the 34 disputed fresh links found 19 weak and 2 wrong; 10 accepted links mixed in as controls were all useful, and the other 49 links were not rechecked. Step 4 is not built, so these results do not show that the clean system can judge an investment view.
clean-arch/PRD.md:578:- open_theses: the identity, question, tracked outcome and period of each thesis already tracked. They tell you where evidence belongs
clean-arch/PRD.md:629:  independently.
clean-arch/PRD.md:1076:Application checks are needed for cross-record and source rules the schema alone cannot enforce: each existing target must resolve to the supplied open list; each new target must resolve to one new thesis in the same answer; each new thesis must be referenced by evidence; local IDs must be unique within their list; each evidence item must have a link; and every evidence item must contain at least one nonempty source passage, and every passage must come from this item. Code retrieves an existing thesis description from the supplied list rather than asking Luna to echo it. These are the intended source and identity relationships, not proof that the source supports every summary claim or that an analyst agrees with the thesis. In the isolated exploratory runner, unmatched passages and unresolved links are retained and explicitly marked as provisional rather than blocking the stream; an unlinked new thesis is not added to the state. Production integration remains open.
clean-arch/PRD.md:1155:| Propose evidence for an existing thesis | **2. Choose a thesis**, existing outcome, uses its supplied identity. Code obtains the description from the supplied list. |
clean-arch/PRD.md:1163:The review covered the visible system prompt, sample input, proposed schema and sample response together, with an independent reader. It found three places needing more than formatting: the thesis bar did not explain which business outcome the question could change; the within-item repeat instruction could hide distinct useful facts; and the output did not distinguish the original party from the reporting speaker. The draft above addresses those points. Its expanded examples teach these distinctions across industries; their added length is intentional for that purpose, not a measured quality improvement.
clean-arch/PRD.md:1167:The assembled clean request and partial forward-run answers now exist in the local trace. This review is not the measured failure-class audit required for an existing measured prompt. The exact thesis-opening boundary, storage mapping for evidence tier and source provenance, handling of ambiguous thesis identity, and compatibility with the existing ledger are still open. The partial run and source contamination do not establish that the draft works end to end.
clean-arch/PRD.md:1214:<thesis identity and description>
clean-arch/PRD.md:1284:| Step 4: thesis judgement | [Judgement](../src/hivemind/views/judging.py), [round orchestration](../src/hivemind/views/round.py) | An existing judgement path can be inspected. What to reuse depends on the clean design's still-open judgement contract. |
clean-arch/PRD.md:1293:This section records the read-only challenge of the clean design, including the independent Claude Code Fable 5.1 review and discussion. The reviewers found no evidence requiring the existing thesis and evidence ledger or its records to be discarded. Reuse still requires checking the actual readers, writers and meaning of the historical relationships. The points below are risks or open contracts unless a specific observation is identified. They are not new product rules or evidence that the proposed system has failed.
clean-arch/PRD.md:1299:5. **What should judgement see?** Two different sources can independently support or contradict one another without being a repeat or revision. Step 2 can save them as different evidence, but whether those relationships must be recorded explicitly or can be understood by Step 4 remains open. Step 4's input and output are not yet designed.
clean-arch/PRD.md:1315:- **Cold start:** preprocess an initial map of entities and their explicit relationships from available sources. The sources, coverage and way to resolve entity identity are open.
clean-arch/PRD.md:1348:- The current slice processes one entity at a time. Cross-entity relationship maintenance remains future design work in section 16. Live Hummingbird integration and Step 4 judgement are not prerequisites for the first Step 1 run.
clean-arch/PRD.md:1353:**Goal:** define the request, references, test-library behavior and failure handling needed for an isolated Step 1 forward run. A complete production writer and reader census is not a prerequisite to this run.
clean-arch/PRD.md:1355:1. Use the field map in section 18 to identify the source data and fields needed for the first run. Keep the meaning of unknown values explicit. A full census of production writers and readers belongs before milestone 3, not before the isolated Step 1 test.
clean-arch/PRD.md:1359:5. Review the complete assembled request and response checks as a unit: instructions, source and open-thesis payload, structured-output schema and field descriptions, followed by the code that reads them. The prompt must not ask for a value the schema or reader cannot represent. Apply the repository's prompt-audit and independent-review rules; the draft has not had a measured clean-design run to audit.
clean-arch/PRD.md:1368:2. **Implement the reviewed handoff.** Supply the article and source fields, relevant company tags, and complete open-thesis identities and descriptions. Consume the reviewed response for existing-thesis evidence, new thesis with evidence, and no result. Keep source passages and attribution. The field mapping and any unresolved thesis-identity boundary must be reviewed in milestone 0.
clean-arch/PRD.md:1370:4. **Advance the test library.** For each article, send the current open-thesis list, capture Luna's proposals, and make the resulting thesis state available to the next article. This is a Step 1 test-library update, not the final production evidence writer or a Jev decision. Use the reviewed identity and reference contract from milestone 0. Start with ordered, one-at-a-time processing so the next item sees the prior result; the existing production selector uses the start-of-day list and cannot simply be assumed to do this.
clean-arch/PRD.md:1404:1. Define Step 4's input, output, timing and treatment of accumulated evidence. This is an open design, not a contract already determined by the diagram.
clean-arch/PRD.md:1406:3. Only after the Step 4 contract is decided, integrate and review judgement using saved evidence. Define the actual Hummingbird event contract and live integration separately; the local source database remains the stand-in until then.
clean-arch/PRD.md:1414:| First Step 1 implementation | Exact thesis–evidence response shape, thesis identity and duplicate definition; how the isolated test library advances. The agreed minimum for a new thesis is entity, question, tracked outcome and period when known. |
clean-arch/PRD.md:1418:| Step 4 and live integration | Judgement contract, source event contract and timing. |
clean-arch/PRD.md:1447:| Evidence and attribution | Evidence includes `id`, `quote`, `one_line`, `speaker`, `original_who`, `original_day`, `tier`, `event_time` and `links`. | Evidence has a response-local ID, source passages, summary, attribution, tier, original statement date and date text, described period and per-link relevance. | Analogous fields need a tested mapping; the described period is not automatically equivalent to `event_time`, and local identity differs from a stored ID. The current writer does not write per-link relevance. In the 49-article exploratory forward trace, 63 of 160 evidence items have multiple passages; one survey item needs its second passage to identify the 60-person population. This confirms that dropping additional passages loses source context in an actual output, while the correct storage and reader mapping is still open. [Handoff evidence and counts](STEP1-QUOTE-INTEGRITY-CENSUS.md). Unknown-date representation also differs. |
clean-arch/PRD.md:1448:| New thesis | A candidate includes the object, question, kind, year, due date, outcome ranges, typed identity fields, opening reason and evidence IDs. | A new proposal has a question, tracked outcome, period when known and response-local ID, referenced by evidence; code supplies the entity. | The existing thesis creation and downstream judgement contracts need additional information that the draft does not supply. Missing dates, outcome ranges, source tiers or research meaning cannot safely be manufactured just to satisfy the old writer. Whether Step 4 needs outcome ranges remains open. |
clean-arch/PRD.md:1457:This is a **design map**, not a built adapter or a decision to preserve every old rule. The current reader examples below are grounded in the linked code; a complete reader and writer census belongs before milestone 3. In the test run, an application-assigned ID is a test-library identity until an eventual ledger write; it is not automatically a production ledger ID.
clean-arch/PRD.md:1466:| Evidence identity and links | Luna uses response-local IDs and attaches typed links with per-thesis relevance; code assigns persistent IDs. | Existing evidence has a local `id` and `links`; the writer assigns stored IDs and writes `evidence_links`. | Verify how response-local IDs become stored IDs and how per-link relevance is retained. Do not collapse records solely because their text is equal. |
clean-arch/PRD.md:1468:| New thesis proposal | Luna returns a question, the outcome to track and a period when known, with evidence; code supplies the current entity. | The current candidate and thesis writer expect object, kind, question, due date, year, rungs and additional typed identity and admission fields. | The minimum is agreed. Verify how it maps to the writer and what judgement requires; whether outcome ranges are needed remains open. Do not invent dates, categories or ranges to force compatibility. An isolated Step 1 test library may track a proposal before those production questions are settled. |
clean-arch/PRD.md:1478:The draft prompt describes the new Step 1 decision, evidence pairing, source attribution and examples, but the **full request and its downstream path are not ready to run**. In particular, the precise thesis identity boundary and what the existing writer and judgement require for a new thesis need further work. The production prompt's opening rules and its later thesis matcher are not automatically part of the clean design. The old production configuration is frozen; the new draft is not a supported production prompt version.
clean-arch/PRD.md:1480:The recommended next task is to finish the Step 1 request and isolated test-library contract, including the open product decisions, and perform the no-cost checks in section 17. The full field-to-reader census and production-compatible writer test are required before milestone 3, not before the first isolated Step 1 run. The new shape must carry the required meaning without inventing fields or silently dropping information.
clean-arch/PRD.md:1488:| 1. Repeat handling | The local snapshot has 2,023 `same_claims` rows for 4,298 evidence records, and the current writer saves a separate linked record. These are model-generated relation labels, not 2,023 independently confirmed duplicates. On 2026-11-06 Alex selected option B: reuse the evidence record and add the new source and missing thesis link. Sections 5, 6 and 13 record the decision and Jev handoff. |
clean-arch/PRD.md:1489:| 2. Multiple thesis links and search | 1,918 of 4,298 stored evidence records link to two or more theses. The nested draft lacked one explicit shared local evidence identity. Section 12 now proposes separate new-thesis and evidence lists, typed links and response-local IDs; code must verify that all links resolve and every new thesis has evidence. This handles one statement linked to multiple theses **within the response**. Alex chose entity-wide evidence search, including for a new thesis; the count does not prove the proposed cross-thesis failure happened. |
clean-arch/PRD.md:1495:| 8. First-run workload | The cited archive has 243 extraction calls averaging about 4,964 input and 1,207 output tokens; median recorded duration is 12.5 seconds. Median times 118 is about 25 minutes, but the first 118 recorded durations sum to about 31 minutes, before any new-run overhead. The clean request may be different. Of the 118 old development articles, one is not marked for the normal feed. These figures are estimates, not a commitment to a run, cost or eligible sample. Section 17 now separates first-run preparation from the later production writer/reader census. |
clean-arch/PRD.md:1499:The pending choices include whether Step 4 needs outcome ranges and what quality the first trace review will establish as sufficient to proceed. The original review's recommended answers on those items are proposals, not decisions. The revised multi-passage prompt and schema have been exercised in an exploratory Luna run, not formally accepted; Jev and the production writing path are not implemented by this clean Step 1 slice.
reports/step2-merge-fix-council-2026-11-09/codex-r1.md:1:**我的建议是先验证“这两条到底重复了哪一句”，不要先加 0.5 门槛，也不要改变现有分组和展示语义。** 目前能确定的是：Step 2 有时把相关但不同的事实当成重复，Step 3 就照这个结果放进同一组。至于模型为什么这么判断，现有记录还不能证明；因此现在还没有经过证明的修复方案。
reports/step2-merge-fix-council-2026-11-09/codex-r1.md:28:**直接过程已成立：**X22 的正确候选被找到；模型对它选择“同一句”，概率 0.49，对“不同”是 0.36；代码按所选类别执行，没有 0.5 下限。Step 3 把新记录放进旧组，保留其自身文字和来源。[保存的决定](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/clean-acceptance-2026-11-09/step2/decisions.json:6723)、[比较代码](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step2/compare.py:109)、[保存代码](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step3/library.py:63)。
reports/step2-merge-fix-council-2026-11-09/codex-r1.md:32:**实际代价：**这条 clean 路径保存了双方原文，并未观察到 X22 的文字被删除。风险在于组标题把新事实说成旧事实的转述，未来读者可能因此忽略新进展；这次运行没有完成 clean Step 4，不能声称已经观察到投资判断受影响。较早的生产路径曾只加来源、丢掉新文字：抽查 40 条重复中有 8 条丢了不同或更丰富的内容，这正是后来保留每条文字的原因。[D204](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:2610)
reports/step2-merge-fix-council-2026-11-09/codex-r1.md:36:- **0.5 门槛：**在新鲜样本这一次的 57 次合组中，它会挡住 X22 的 0.49，也会挡住 X47 的 0.47。X47 两篇都报告 Broadcom 对相同财年的 1,150 亿和 2,300 亿美元预测，后文增加了原先预测；两位读者都接受合组。开发样本的明确错误也有 0.53、0.84、0.51，门槛挡不住。历史上 0.6 的试验也同时挡住正确合并。[X47 原文](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/clean-acceptance-2026-11-09/step2/merge-packet-2.json:849)、[历史检查](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1131)。所以这不是已证明的根本修复；不能把被挡住的记录自动说成“不同”。
reports/step2-merge-fix-council-2026-11-09/stored-census.md:1:# c8 stored merge census, 2026-11-09
reports/step2-merge-fix-council-2026-11-09/stored-census.md:3:Read-only count of decision outcome `kind == repeat`, linked by exact record ids to `merge-key.json`, `merge-answer-MA-*`, `merge-answer-MB-*` and `merge-third-answer.json`. All 363 selected pairs were identifiable and each had two first-reader judgments; all disputes carried their third-reader verdict. This is one fresh draw and three draws over the same 115-article dev set, not 363 independent events.
reports/step2-merge-fix-council-2026-11-09/stored-census.md:12:Four wrong merge instances by recorded readers comprise 3 different source-pair classes (one repeats in 2 dev draws): (1) production/20% forecast vs August customer deliveries, Jev 0.49; (2) analyst revenue-level forecasts vs growth rate forecasts, 0.53; (3) cloud providers' revenue share vs developers' sales share, 0.84 and 0.51. These have shared topic but distinct claims according to the readers; X22 was also called overlap by an independent blind Claude, so classification uncertainty remains. The developer-sales case has additional source-fidelity ambiguity: it is not proven that the media report accurately represented its source. See saved `library.json` group original and member and `decisions.json` at each run; see prior X22 council report.
reports/step2-merge-fix-council-2026-11-09/stored-census.md:14:## 0.5 threshold counterexample
reports/step2-merge-fix-council-2026-11-09/stored-census.md:16:Among the 363 merges, six selected same-statement scores are below 0.5: fresh X22 (0.49, wrong), fresh X47 (0.47, accepted by both), dev1 X33 (0.47, overlap), dev2 X27 (0.41, accepted by both), dev3 X26 (0.41, accepted by both), dev3 X28 (0.49, accepted by both). Rejecting these merges on a 0.5 floor would remove 1 of the 4 wrong instances, while splitting 4 accepted and 1 overlap instance. These are fixed archived outcomes; the statement is conditional on this isolated transformation and neither estimates behavior of a forward run nor argues any threshold is appropriate.

